;; std/sse.blsp — Server-Sent Events (text/event-stream): a client + server framing.
;;
;; Design notes (the *why*; the *what* is in the docstrings below):
;;  - SSE is **not** request→response like `request`: the server holds the response
;;    open and *pushes* frames down it. So it gets its own module (reusing `http`'s URL,
;;    header and request-head helpers for the handshake) and its own **process**:
;;    `connect` spawns a reader that streams `[:tcp …]` chunks and `send`s each parsed
;;    event to a subscriber's mailbox — the same deliver-to-a-mailbox shape as `gui/open`
;;    input (ADR-058) and the observer's remote agent.
;;  - **Any method, any body, http or https.** A browser `EventSource` is a GET, but the
;;    streaming APIs worth talking to (OpenAI-style chat completions with `stream: true`)
;;    are a POST with a JSON body and an `Authorization` header, over TLS. `connect` takes
;;    `{:method :headers :body :ca}` and opens `https://` through `tls/request`, exactly as
;;    `http/request` does, so one reader serves both.
;;  - **HTTP/1.0, deliberately.** A 1.1 server may answer `Transfer-Encoding: chunked`, and
;;    chunk-size lines are counted in BYTES and may fall between the bytes of one UTF-8
;;    character — which a text-mode read (below) would decode as garbage before any Brood
;;    code saw the framing. A 1.0 response cannot be chunked: the body is the plain byte
;;    stream until the server closes, which is also exactly when the stream ends. Same
;;    choice, same reason, as `http/request` (the head is `http/request-head`).
;;  - **A refused stream still reports its body.** A non-2xx status is how an API says
;;    "bad key", "rate limited", "no such model" — and it says it in the body. The reader
;;    collects that body (to close, `Content-Length`, a cap, or a timeout) and ends with
;;    `[:sse-closed {:status :headers :body}]`, so a caller can show the provider's own
;;    message. Every connection ends with exactly ONE `[:sse-closed …]`.
;;  - **Pure frame parsing** (the `observe-frame` discipline): the wire grammar
;;    (`event:`/`data:`/`id:` field lines, blank-line frame boundaries, comment lines,
;;    `\n`/`\r\n`/`\r` terminators — a `\r\n` split across two reads included) is parsed by
;;    pure functions (`feed`, `parse-event`, `frames`, `split-head`, `parse-head`) that are
;;    unit-testable without a socket; only the reader process touches IO.
;;  - **Integrates with `ui-run` via `with-events`** (in `std/editor/ui.blsp`): wrap a
;;    display so its `:poll` surfaces queued `[:sse …]` messages as loop input — so
;;    pushed events drive the UI on the terminal *and* the GUI.
;;  - **Text-mode reads are deliberate** (ADR-141): SSE is a UTF-8 *text* protocol
;;    (`text/event-stream`), and the kernel's text decode — longest valid prefix per
;;    chunk, split multibyte characters carried to the next read, over TCP and TLS alike —
;;    is exactly the right framing for it, so events surface as strings with no decode
;;    step here. Binary-mode `bytes` reads are for *binary* protocols (see http's request
;;    path).
;;  - **The reader dies with its subscriber.** It `monitor`s the subscriber and closes the
;;    socket when that process ends, so a stopped consumer stops the stream (and, for a
;;    metered API, the bill) without having to remember the reader's pid.
;;  - Opt-in (`require`), never in the prelude.

(defmodule sse
  "Server-Sent Events (`text/event-stream`) in Brood: a client, and server-side
framing for pushing events over `http`'s streaming seam.

    (def conn (connect \"https://api.example.com/v1/chat/completions\" (self)
                {:method \"POST\"
                 :headers {\"Authorization\" \"Bearer …\" \"Content-Type\" \"application/json\"}
                 :body (json/encode {:model \"m\" :stream true :messages […]})}))
    (receive ([:sse ev] ev))     ; => {:event \"message\" :data \"{…}\"}
    (exit conn :kill)            ; disconnect

`connect` spawns a reader **process** that opens the stream and delivers each
event to `subscriber`'s mailbox as `[:sse {:event :data :id}]` (`[:sse-open status]`
once a 2xx head arrives, then exactly one `[:sse-closed reason]` when the link ends —
`reason` is `{:status :headers :body}` when the server refused with a non-2xx status);
it returns that process's pid (kill it to disconnect; it also stops by itself when the
subscriber exits). The `:data` is the raw payload — `parse` it yourself. Pair with
`with-events` (std/editor/ui.blsp) to feed events into a `ui-run` loop.

On the server side, `headers` / `frame` / `emit` turn payloads into wire
frames: return `(http/stream-response 200 (headers) (fn (sock) … (emit sock
data) …))` from an `http` handler to push events to a browser `EventSource`."
  (:use http))

;; The reader consumes UNTRUSTED server output. A malicious endpoint that never
;; sends the head's blank line, or an event with no `\n\n` frame boundary, would
;; grow the reader's buffer without bound → OOM. Cap the buffered head/leftover at
;; 16 MiB (SSE frames are tiny; this is purely a runaway guard); past it the
;; reader closes the socket and reports `[:sse-closed :buffer-overflow]`.
(def *max-buffer-bytes* (* 16 1024 1024))

;; A refused stream's body is an error message, not a download: keep at most this much of
;; it, and stop waiting for the rest after this long (a server that answers 4xx and then
;; holds the connection open must not park the reader forever).
(def *max-error-body-bytes* (* 1024 1024))
(def *error-body-timeout-ms* 30000)

;; ---- pure frame parsing -----------------------------------------------------

(defn- normalize (s)
  "Normalise line terminators to `\\n` (SSE allows `\\r\\n`, `\\r`, or `\\n`)."
  (string/replace (string/replace s "\r\n" "\n") "\r" "\n"))

(defn- frames (buf)
  "`[complete-event-blocks remainder]` extracted from accumulated stream text `buf`."
  ;; ONE native split, not a `substring` of the rest per block. The drain this replaced
  ;; re-copied the remainder of the buffer for every event, so a single socket read holding
  ;; k events cost O(k * chunk) — the `stream-lines` quadratic (2026-08-02) in another
  ;; module, and one 64 KB read is the normal case, not the pathological one.
  ;; `string-split` keeps a trailing empty piece, so the LAST piece is exactly the
  ;; remainder (`""` when the buffer ended on a blank line); an empty buffer splits to no
  ;; pieces at all, hence the guard. Covered by the `sse frames-1c` sweep row.
  (let (parts (string/split buf "\n\n"))
    (if (empty? parts) [nil ""] [(seq/drop-last parts 1) (last parts)])))

(defn- field (ev line)
  "Fold one SSE field `line` into event map `ev`. `data` lines accumulate (joined by
`\\n`); `event`/`id`/`retry` set their field; comment lines (`:`-prefixed) and blanks
are ignored. One optional space after the colon is stripped."
  (cond
    (= line "") ev
    (string/starts-with? line ":") ev
    else
      (let (i (index-of line ":")
            field (if (< i 0) line (string/substring line 0 i))
            raw (if (< i 0) "" (string/substring line (+ i 1) (string/length line)))
            val (if (string/starts-with? raw " ") (string/substring raw 1 (string/length raw)) raw))
        (cond
          (= field "data")
            (assoc ev
              :data (if (nil? (get ev :data)) val (str (get ev :data) "\n" val)))
          (= field "event") (assoc ev :event val)
          (= field "id") (assoc ev :id val)
          (= field "retry") (assoc ev :retry val)
          else ev))))

(defn- parse-event (block)
  "Parse one event `block` (its field lines) into `{:event :data :id …}`, defaulting
the type to `\"message\"` and the data to `\"\"` (the SSE spec's defaults). Pure."
  (let (ev (fold (string/split block "\n") {} field))
    (assoc ev :event (or (get ev :event) "message") :data (or (get ev :data) ""))))

(defn- feed (state chunk)
  "Fold one raw text `chunk` off the wire into the reader's `state` — `[buffer carry]`,
the normalised text not yet ending in a blank line and a held-back trailing `\\r` — and
return `[events next-state]`, `events` the parsed complete events in order. Pure.

The `\\r` is held because a `\\r\\n` can arrive split across two reads: normalising the
`\\r` alone would read it as a line end and the `\\n` after it as a second one — a blank
line, which ends an event in the middle."
  (let ([buffer carry] state
        raw (str carry chunk)
        held (if (string/ends-with? raw "\r") "\r" "")
        whole (if (= held "") raw (string/substring raw 0 (- (string/length raw) 1)))
        [blocks remainder] (frames (str buffer (normalize whole))))
    [(map (seq/reject blocks (fn (block) (= block ""))) parse-event) [remainder held]]))

(defn- split-head (text)
  "`[head rest]` when `text` holds a complete response head (through its blank line —
`\\r\\n\\r\\n`, or a bare `\\n\\n` from a lax server), else nil. Pure."
  (let (crlf (index-of text "\r\n\r\n")
        lf (index-of text "\n\n"))
    (cond
      (and (>= crlf 0) (or (< lf 0) (< crlf lf)))
        [(string/substring text 0 crlf) (string/substring text (+ crlf 4) (string/length text))]
      (>= lf 0) [(string/substring text 0 lf) (string/substring text (+ lf 2) (string/length text))]
      else nil)))

(defn- parse-head (head)
  "`{:status :headers}` from a response `head` (the lines before the blank line): the
numeric status (0 when the status line does not parse) and the headers, names
lower-cased. Pure."
  (let (lines (string/split (normalize head) "\n")
        parts (string/split (or (first lines) "") " ")
        n (string/->number (nth parts 1 "0")))
    ;; `failure?`, not `or`: a malformed status line parses to a TRUTHY failure, so
    ;; `(or n 0)` would put a `#failure{…}` where the status code belongs.
    {:status (if (failure? n) 0 n)
     :headers (fold (rest lines)
                {}
                (fn (headers line)
                  (let (i (index-of line ":"))
                    (if (< i 0)
                      headers
                      (assoc headers
                        (string/lower (string/trim (string/substring line 0 i)))
                          (string/trim (string/substring line (+ i 1) (string/length line))))))))}))

(defn- success? (status) (and (>= status 200) (< status 300)))

(defn- declared-length (headers)
  ;; A usable Content-Length is a non-negative integer; anything else means "read to close".
  (let (n (string/->number (or (get headers "content-length") "")))
    (if (and (int? n) (>= n 0)) n nil)))

;; ---- server-side framing (the inverse of the parser above) ------------------
;; Pair with `http`'s streaming seam: a handler returns `(stream-response 200
;; (headers) (fn (sock) … (emit sock data) …))`, and these turn payloads
;; into wire frames. Pure string building over `tcp/send` — the kernel adds nothing.

(defn headers ()
  "Headers for an event stream: `text/event-stream`, no caching, keep-alive. Pass as
the `headers` of a `stream-response` so a browser `EventSource` reads the open stream."
  {"Content-Type" "text/event-stream" "Cache-Control" "no-cache" "Connection" "keep-alive"})

(defn frame (data &optional (event nil))
  "One SSE frame for `data`: every line prefixed `data: ` (so multi-line payloads
round-trip), an optional `event:` type line first, terminated by the blank line.
The exact inverse of `parse-event`."
  (str (if (nil? event) "" (str "event: " event "\n"))
    (string/join (map (string/split data "\n") (fn (l) (str "data: " l))) "\n")
    "\n\n"))

(defn emit (sock data &optional (event nil))
  "Push one SSE event (`data`, with an optional `event` type) down `sock` — the live
socket a `stream-response` handler was handed. Just `(tcp/send sock (frame …))`."
  (tcp/send sock (frame data event)))

;; ---- the reader process (IO) ------------------------------------------------
;; Every receive below also watches `watch`, the monitor on the subscriber: when the
;; subscriber exits, the reader closes the socket and ends without a word (there is
;; nobody left to tell).

(defn- stream (sock subscriber watch state)
  "Wait for the next chunk, deliver every event it completes to `subscriber`, recurse.
Ends with `[:sse-closed reason]` when the socket closes or errors. Tail-recursive."
  (receive
    ([:tcp ^sock d] (let ([events next] (feed state d))
                      (doseq (ev events)
                        (send subscriber [:sse ev]))
                      (if (> (string/length (first next)) *max-buffer-bytes*)
                        (do (tcp/close sock) (send subscriber [:sse-closed :buffer-overflow]))
                        (stream sock subscriber watch next))))
    ([:tcp-closed ^sock] (send subscriber [:sse-closed :closed]))
    ([:tcp-error ^sock msg] (send subscriber [:sse-closed msg]))
    ([:down ^watch _ _] (tcp/close sock))))

(defn- refused (sock subscriber head chunks)
  ;; The one message a non-2xx stream ends with: its status, headers and body text.
  (tcp/close sock)
  (send subscriber
    [:sse-closed (assoc head :body (apply str (reverse chunks)))]))

(defn- read-refusal (sock subscriber watch head chunks have)
  "Collect a non-2xx response's body — `chunks` in reverse, `have` their byte total — to
close, `Content-Length`, the cap or the timeout, then report it with `refused`."
  (let (want (declared-length (get head :headers)))
    (if (or (> have *max-error-body-bytes*) (and want (>= have want)))
      (refused sock subscriber head chunks)
      (receive
        ([:tcp ^sock d] (read-refusal sock
                          subscriber
                          watch
                          head
                          (cons d chunks)
                          (+ have (bytes/length (string/->bytes d)))))
        ([:tcp-closed ^sock] (refused sock subscriber head chunks))
        ([:tcp-error ^sock _] (refused sock subscriber head chunks))
        ([:down ^watch _ _] (tcp/close sock))
        (after *error-body-timeout-ms* (refused sock subscriber head chunks))))))

(defn- read-head (sock subscriber watch buf)
  "Read until the HTTP response head's blank line. A 2xx status sends `[:sse-open
status]` and hands the leftover text to `stream`; any other status reads the body
for the refusal."
  (receive
    ([:tcp ^sock d] (let (text (str buf d)
                          split (split-head text))
                      (cond
                        (and (nil? split) (> (string/length text) *max-buffer-bytes*))
                          (do
                            (tcp/close sock)
                            (send subscriber [:sse-closed :buffer-overflow]))
                        (nil? split) (read-head sock subscriber watch text)
                        else
                          (let ([head-text rest] split
                                head (parse-head head-text))
                            (if (success? (get head :status))
                              (let ([events state] (feed ["" ""] rest))
                                (send subscriber [:sse-open (get head :status)])
                                (doseq (ev events)
                                  (send subscriber [:sse ev]))
                                (stream sock subscriber watch state))
                              (read-refusal sock
                                subscriber
                                watch
                                head
                                (list rest)
                                (bytes/length (string/->bytes rest))))))))
    ([:tcp-closed ^sock] (send subscriber [:sse-closed :closed]))
    ([:tcp-error ^sock msg] (send subscriber [:sse-closed msg]))
    ([:down ^watch _ _] (tcp/close sock))))

(defn- open-socket (u payload ca)
  ;; The two transports, as in `http/request`: TLS sends the request with the
  ;; handshake, plain TCP connects then sends. Both stay in TEXT mode (see the notes).
  (if (= (get u :scheme) "https")
    (tls/request (get u :host) (get u :port) payload ca)
    (let (sock (tcp/connect (get u :host) (get u :port)))
      (tcp/send sock payload)
      sock)))

(defn- run-reader (u payload ca subscriber)
  ;; A connect that fails outright (refused, unresolvable) raises here; it becomes the
  ;; stream's one `[:sse-closed …]` rather than a reader that dies unheard.
  (let (watch (monitor subscriber)
        opened (try
                 [:ok (open-socket u payload ca)]
                 (catch problem [:error (error-message problem)])))
    (match opened
      ([:ok sock] (read-head sock subscriber watch ""))
      ([:error message] (send subscriber [:sse-closed message])))))

(defn connect (url subscriber &optional (opts {}))
  "Open an SSE stream to `url` (`http://` or `https://`) and deliver its events to
`subscriber`'s mailbox. `opts` is `{:method :headers :body :ca}`: the method (default
`\"GET\"`), extra request headers (over `Accept: text/event-stream`, `Cache-Control:
no-cache`, Host / User-Agent / Connection, any of them overridable), a body (string,
`bytes` or iolist — Content-Length is its byte count), and for https a PEM `:ca`
replacing the Mozilla roots (a private CA, a `tls/self-signed` dev server).

The subscriber receives, in order:
- `[:sse-open status]` once a 2xx response head arrives;
- `[:sse {:event :data :id}]` per event (`:event` defaults to `\"message\"`, `:data` is
  the raw payload, multi-line data joined by `\\n`);
- exactly one `[:sse-closed reason]` at the end: `:closed` (the server closed the
  stream), `:buffer-overflow`, a transport error string (refused, TLS failure), or —
  when the server answered a non-2xx status, with no `:sse-open` before it —
  `{:status :headers :body}`, the body as text (a JSON API's error message).

Runs in a **new process** (so it parks on `receive`, holding no worker) and returns
its pid; `(exit pid :kill)` disconnects, and the reader also closes the stream by itself
when `subscriber` exits. A bad URL or a CR/LF in the method, target or a header raises
here, in the caller. Sent as HTTP/1.0 (see the module notes)."
  (let (u (http/parse-url url)
        body (bytes/concat (or (get opts :body) ""))
        headers (http/request-headers u
                  body
                  (merge {"Accept" "text/event-stream" "Cache-Control" "no-cache"}
                    (or (get opts :headers) {})))
        head (http/request-head (or (get opts :method) "GET") u headers)
        ca (get opts :ca))
    (when (not (includes? ["http" "https"] (get u :scheme)))
      (error "connect: not an http or https url: " url))
    (spawn (run-reader u [head body] ca subscriber))))
